       The  mmoovvee__mmoouunntt()  system call is part of the suite of file‐descriptor‐
       based mount facilities in Linux.

       mmoovvee__mmoouunntt()  moves  the  mount  object  indicated  by  _f_r_o_m___d_i_r_f_d  and
       _f_r_o_m___p_a_t_h to the path indicated by _t_o___d_i_r_f_d and _t_o___p_a_t_h.  The mount ob‐
       ject  being  moved  can be an existing mount point in the current mount
       namespace,  or  a  detached  mount  object  created  by  ffssmmoouunntt(2)  or
       ooppeenn__ttrreeee(2) with OOPPEENN__TTRREEEE__CCLLOONNEE.

       To  access  the  source mount object or the destination mount point, no
       permissions are required on the object itself, but if  either  pathname
       is  supplied, execute (search) permission is required on all of the di‐
       rectories specified in _f_r_o_m___p_a_t_h or _t_o___p_a_t_h.

       The calling process must have the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability in order  to
       move or attach a mount object.

       As  with  "*at()"  system  calls,  mmoovvee__mmoouunntt() uses the _f_r_o_m___d_i_r_f_d and
       _t_o___d_i_r_f_d arguments in conjunction with the _f_r_o_m___p_a_t_h and _t_o___p_a_t_h  argu‐
       ments  to  determine  the  source and destination objects to operate on
       (respectively), as follows:

       •  If the pathname given in *___p_a_t_h is absolute, then the  corresponding
          *___d_i_r_f_d is ignored.

       •  If  the  pathname  given in *___p_a_t_h is relative and the corresponding
          *___d_i_r_f_d is the special value AATT__FFDDCCWWDD, then  *___p_a_t_h  is  interpreted
          relative  to  the  current  working directory of the calling process
          (like ooppeenn(2)).

       •  If the pathname given in *___p_a_t_h is relative, then it is  interpreted
          relative  to the directory referred to by the corresponding file de‐
          scriptor *___d_i_r_f_d (rather than relative to the current working direc‐
          tory of the calling process, as is done by ooppeenn(2)  for  a  relative
          pathname).  In this case, the corresponding *___d_i_r_f_d must be a direc‐
          tory  that  was  opened  for  reading (OO__RRDDOONNLLYY) or using the OO__PPAATTHH
          flag.

       •  If *___p_a_t_h is an empty string, and  _f_l_a_g_s  contains  the  appropriate
          MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT___*__EEMMPPTTYY__PPAATTHH flag, then the corresponding file descriptor
          *___d_i_r_f_d  is  operated  on directly.  In this case, the corresponding
          *___d_i_r_f_d may refer to any type of file, not just a directory.

       See ooppeennaatt(2) for an explanation of why the *___d_i_r_f_d arguments are  use‐
       ful.

       _f_l_a_g_s  can  be  used to control aspects of the path lookup for both the
       source and destination objects, as well  as  other  properties  of  the
       mount  operation.   A  value  for _f_l_a_g_s is constructed by bitwise ORing
       zero or more of the following constants:

              MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__FF__EEMMPPTTYY__PPAATTHH
                     If _f_r_o_m___p_a_t_h is an empty string, operate on the file  re‐
                     ferred  to  by  _f_r_o_m___d_i_r_f_d  (which may have been obtained
                     from ooppeenn(2),  ffssmmoouunntt(2),  or  ooppeenn__ttrreeee(2)).   In  this
                     case,  _f_r_o_m___d_i_r_f_d may refer to any type of file, not just
                     a directory.  If  _f_r_o_m___d_i_r_f_d  is  AATT__FFDDCCWWDD,  mmoovvee__mmoouunntt()
                     will  operate  on  the  current  working directory of the
                     calling process.

                     This is the most common mechanism used to attach detached
                     mount objects produced by ffssmmoouunntt(2) and ooppeenn__ttrreeee(2)  to
                     a mount point.

              MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__TT__EEMMPPTTYY__PPAATTHH
                     As  with  MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__FF__EEMMPPTTYY__PPAATTHH,  except  operating  on
                     _t_o___d_i_r_f_d and _t_o___p_a_t_h.

              MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__FF__SSYYMMLLIINNKKSS
                     If _f_r_o_m___p_a_t_h references a symbolic link, then dereference
                     it.  The default behaviour for  mmoovvee__mmoouunntt()  is  to  _n_o_t
                     _f_o_l_l_o_w symbolic links.

              MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__TT__SSYYMMLLIINNKKSS
                     As   with   MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__FF__SSYYMMLLIINNKKSS,  except  operating  on
                     _t_o___d_i_r_f_d and _t_o___p_a_t_h.

              MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__FF__NNOO__AAUUTTOOMMOOUUNNTT
                     Do not automount the terminal ("basename")  component  of
                     _f_r_o_m___p_a_t_h  if  it  is  a  directory  that is an automount
                     point.  This allows a mount object that has an  automount
                     point  at  its  root  to be moved and prevents unintended
                     triggering of an automount point.  This flag has  no  ef‐
                     fect  if  the  automount  point  has already been mounted
                     over.

              MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__TT__NNOO__AAUUTTOOMMOOUUNNTT
                     As with MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__FF__NNOO__AAUUTTOOMMOOUUNNTT,  except  operating  on
                     _t_o___d_i_r_f_d  and _t_o___p_a_t_h.  This allows an automount point to
                     be manually mounted over.

              MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__SSEETT__GGRROOUUPP (since Linux 5.15)
                     Add the attached private‐propagation mount  object  indi‐
                     cated  by _t_o___d_i_r_f_d and _t_o___p_a_t_h into the mount propagation
                     "peer  group"  of  the  attached  non‐private‐propagation
                     mount object indicated by _f_r_o_m___d_i_r_f_d and _f_r_o_m___p_a_t_h.

                     Unlike other mmoovvee__mmoouunntt() operations, this operation does
                     not  move  or attach any mount objects.  Instead, it only
                     updates the metadata of attached mount  objects.   (Also,
                     take  careful note of the argument order—the mount object
                     being modified by this operation is the one specified  by
                     _t_o___d_i_r_f_d and _t_o___p_a_t_h.)

                     This  makes it possible to first create a mount tree con‐
                     sisting only of private mounts and then configure the de‐
                     sired propagation layout afterwards.   (See  the  "SHARED
                     SUBTREES"  section of mmoouunntt__nnaammeessppaacceess(7) for more infor‐
                     mation about mount propagation and peer groups.)

              MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__BBEENNEEAATTHH (since Linux 6.5)
                     If the path indicated by _t_o___d_i_r_f_d and _t_o___p_a_t_h is  an  ex‐
                     isting  mount object, rather than attaching or moving the
                     mount object indicated by _f_r_o_m___d_i_r_f_d and _f_r_o_m___p_a_t_h on top
                     of the mount stack, attach or move it beneath the current
                     top mount on the mount stack.

                     After  using  MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__BBEENNEEAATTHH,  it  is   possible   to
                     uummoouunntt(2)  the top mount in order to reveal the mount ob‐
                     ject which was attached beneath it earlier.  This  allows
                     for  the  seamless  (and atomic) replacement of intricate
                     mount trees, which can further be  used  to  "upgrade"  a
                     mount tree with a newer version.

                     This operation has several restrictions:

                     •  Mount  objects cannot be attached beneath the filesys‐
                        tem root, including cases where  the  filesystem  root
                        was  configured  by  cchhrroooott(2)  or  ppiivvoott__rroooott(2).  To
                        mount beneath the filesystem root, ppiivvoott__rroooott(2)  must
                        be used.

                     •  The target path indicated by _t_o___d_i_r_f_d and _t_o___p_a_t_h must
                        not be a detached mount object, such as those produced
                        by ooppeenn__ttrreeee(2) with OOPPEENN__TTRREEEE__CCLLOONNEE or ffssmmoouunntt(2).

                     •  The current top mount of the target path’s mount stack
                        and  its parent mount must be in the calling process’s
                        mount namespace.

                     •  The caller must have sufficient privileges to  unmount
                        the  top  mount  of  the target path’s mount stack, to
                        prove they have privileges to  reveal  the  underlying
                        mount.

                     •  Mount    propagation    events   triggered   by   this
                        mmoovvee__mmoouunntt() operation (as  described  in  mmoouunntt__nnaammee‐‐
                        ssppaacceess(7)) are calculated based on the parent mount of
                        the  current  top  mount  of  the  target path’s mount
                        stack.

                     •  The target path’s mount cannot be an ancestor  in  the
                        mount tree of the source mount object.

                     •  The  source mount object must not have any overmounts,
                        otherwise it  would  be  possible  to  create  "shadow
                        mounts"  (i.e.,  two mounts mounted on the same parent
                        mount at the same mount point).

                     •  It is not possible to move a mount beneath a top mount
                        if the parent mount of the current  top  mount  propa‐
                        gates   to   the   top   mount   itself.    Otherwise,
                        MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__BBEENNEEAATTHH would cause the mount object to  be
                        propagated to the top mount from the parent mount, de‐
                        feating the purpose of using MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__BBEENNEEAATTHH.

                     •  It is not possible to move a mount beneath a top mount
                        if  the  parent  mount of the current top mount propa‐
                        gates to the mount object being mounted beneath.  Oth‐
                        erwise, this would cause a similar  propagation  issue
                        to  the  previous point, also defeating the purpose of
                        using MMOOVVEE__MMOOUUNNTT__BBEENNEEAATTHH.

       If _f_r_o_m___d_i_r_f_d is a mount object file descriptor and mmoovvee__mmoouunntt() is op‐
       erating on it directly, _f_r_o_m___d_i_r_f_d  will  remain  associated  with  the
       mount  object  after  mmoovvee__mmoouunntt()  succeeds, so you may repeatedly use
       _f_r_o_m___d_i_r_f_d with mmoovvee__mmoouunntt(2) and/or "*at()" system calls as many times
       as necessary.

